The original does a linear scan over every message definition on each call. This version builds a 65,536-entry lookup table once, indexed directly by message number, so each later call is a single array read.

- **Same results:** the table is filled only from the constant `mesgs` array, and a slot is written only if it is still empty. If two entries share a number, the first one wins, as in the original scan.
- **No mutable global state:** the table depends only on constant data, not on anything that can change at runtime.
- **Thread-safe setup:** it is a function-local static, so C++ guarantees it is initialized once even if several threads call in at the same time.
- **Fallback:** if `MESGS` were ever 0xFFFF or more, the index could collide with the empty marker, so the code keeps the original linear scan in that case.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Table
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        Table()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (idx[n] == 0xFFFF)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };

    if (MESGS >= 0xFFFF)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            if (mesgs[i].num == num)
                return &mesgs[i];
        }
        return NULL;
    }

    static const Table table;
    FIT_UINT16 i = table.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```